            <ArticleTitle>Local anesthetic reduces the pain of colposcopic biopsies: a randomized trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1164-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study was undertaken to determine whether administration of local anesthetic reduces pain associated with colposcopically directed cervical biopsies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fifty-six patients undergoing colposcopically directed cervical biopsies were randomly selected prospectively to receive a 1% lidocaine injection or no injection before the biopsy procedure. Pain was assessed by using a 10-cm visual analog scale at various points during the procedure. Data were analyzed with the Student t test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Injection of lidocaine resulted in a reduction in pain scores for cervical biopsies (4.004 to 1.166, P: &lt;.001), endocervical curettage (5.757 to 3.900, P =.0018), and overall procedure scores (4.911 to 3.145, P =.0013).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This pilot study demonstrates that injection of local anesthetic at the site of colposcopically direct cervical biopsies decreases the pain perceived by patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
